<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tendai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded 806</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Tendai-shū, Tendai Dharma Flower School, Hokkeshū</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tendai is a Mahāyāna Buddhist school with significant esoteric elements, officially established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 806 by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> and based since then on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, where he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>. Drawn from the Chinese Tiantai tradition and its patriarch Zhiyi, it emphasises the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> and holds that all the Buddha's teachings can be unified in one perfect system. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> it eclipsed the Hossō school and rivalled Shingon as the most influential sect at the imperial court, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period its temples held vast lands and fielded warrior monks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Hōnen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogen">Dōgen</a> all trained as Tendai monks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> destroyed Enryaku-ji in 1571.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tendai.jpg" alt="Tendai" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hiroaki Kaneko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:桜と比叡山延暦寺_(Enryaku-ji_with_Cherry_Blossoms)_28_Apr,_2013_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Teachings of the Chinese Tiantai school had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as early as 754 with Jianzhen, but took root in an organised school only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> joined the Japanese mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 804 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>. Seven days after his death the court allowed Tendai its own bodhisattva precept ordination, separate from the powerful schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, and Gishin, the first head of the order, presided over the first ordinations in 827. Ryōgen, the 18th abbot of Enryaku-ji, was close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, and through him Tendai became dominant at the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p>He is said to have hired an army to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, though some scholars attribute the rise of warrior monks to the decline of the imperial bureaucracy and the growth of temple estates. Tendai monks attacked the Hongan-ji in 1465, and the school took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180 to 1185. In the Edo period Tendai was folded into the Tokugawa temple registration system, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a> was rebuilt with shogunal support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Philosophically Tendai kept the beliefs of Chinese Tiantai, founded on the doctrines of Zhiyi and Zhanran, such as the infinite lifespan of the Buddha, the buddha-nature of all beings and the 'three thousand realms in a single thought moment'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> also brought Zen, Pure Land, esoteric and Vinaya elements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and his heirs Ennin, Enchin and Annen promoted esoteric practice.</p><p>Senior teachers train in the Four Integrated Schools of perfect teaching, esotericism, meditation and precepts. The school ordains with the bodhisattva precepts drawn from the Brahmajala Sutra, with no monastic Pratimokṣa, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> defended in his Kenkairon, and which did not distinguish monastics from laypersons. Its esoteric branch is called Taimitsu, against Shingon's Tōmitsu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tendai held that all beings have buddha-nature, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saicho">Saichō</a> into debate with the Hossō scholar Tokuitsu, whose school held that some beings lack the seeds of buddhahood. The medieval school was where the doctrine of hongaku, innate enlightenment, developed, and the theory of honji suijaku, which treated the kami as traces of buddhas, helped join native Japanese religion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Hōnen, Eisai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogen">Dōgen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a> were all trained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> before founding schools of their own in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period. The Meiji government's separation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> from Buddhism led to the confiscation of temple lands and a decline in patronage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tendai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Japan, Saichō (767 to 822) brought Tiantai as the <b>Tendai</b> school.</blockquote><a href="/work/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tendai</b>, brought from China by the monk Saichō, rested on the Lotus Sutra and taught that enlightenment was open to “every creature”; its monastery on Mount Hiei, above the capital, grew close to the court and taught great reverence for the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
